OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026", "title": "Deterministic quasipolynomial-time mean-payoff games", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Deterministic-quasipolynomial-time-mean-payoff-games-September-25-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "104", "family_title": "Quasipolynomial algorithms for mean-payoff, stochastic and parity games", "abstract_html": "We give a deterministic algorithm that computes the complete zero-threshold winning set of a finite mean-payoff game with arbitrary signed integer edge weights encoded in binary. For total explicit input length <i>L</i>, it uses \\(2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}\\) bit operations. A reduction also computes the exact rational value at every vertex and globally optimal positional strategies for both players within the same quasipolynomial bound.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G=(V,E)\\) be a finite directed graph with \\(V=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), \\(n\\ge 1\\), and at least one outgoing edge at every vertex.</p><p class=\"li\">The ownership sets \\(V_{\\mathrm{Max}}\\) and \\(V_{\\mathrm{Min}}\\) partition \\(V\\). At a vertex, its owner chooses the next edge.</p><p class=\"li\">The weights \\(w:E\\to\\mathbb Z\\) are arbitrary signed binary integers. Self-loops and parallel edges are allowed; parallel edges are separate entries of the explicit edge list.</p><p class=\"li\">Both players’ strategies may depend on the entire finite history. For an infinite play \\(\\pi=(e_0,e_1,\\ldots)\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{MP}_w(\\pi)\n  =\n  \\liminf_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac1T\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}w(e_t).\n  \\]\n  Write \\(\\pi_{v,\\sigma,\\tau}\\) for the play induced from \\(v\\) by Max strategy \\(\\sigma\\) and Min strategy \\(\\tau\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be the complete input length in a fixed conventional binary encoding of the graph, endpoints, ownership, and weights.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A uniform deterministic algorithm returns exactly\n\\[\nU=\n\\left\\{\nv\\in V:\n\\exists\\text{ Max strategy }\\sigma\\\n\\forall\\text{ Min strategies }\\tau,\\ \n\\operatorname{MP}_w(\\pi_{v,\\sigma,\\tau})\\ge 0\n\\right\\}.\n\\]\nFor an absolute constant \\(C&gt;0\\), the complete computation uses at most\n\\[\n2^{C(\\log_2(L+2))^2}\n\\]\nbit operations in a standard model polynomially equivalent to a uniform deterministic Turing machine. The bound includes preprocessing, exact arithmetic, recursion, and output of the entire set \\(U\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Compute the transformed weights and the initial data\n\\[\nA=0,\\qquad D=D_0,\\qquad a_i=b_i=1/n,\n\\]\nwhere \\(D_0\\) is the width in Proposition 2. Run \\(\\mathrm{Label}\\) and output its positively labelled vertices.</p><p>Every support has mass at most one under these initial masses. The box solution \\(h\\) supplied by Proposition 1 is both a Max witness and a Min witness. Moreover,\n\\[\nP=D_0/32\\ge nW.\n\\]\nIts Max claims therefore include every high vertex of Proposition 2, and its Min claims include every low vertex.</p><p>Proposition 5 supplies all those labels simultaneously. Proposition 2 states that the bands partition \\(V\\), with Max winning throughout the high band and Min forcing upper limiting average at most \\(-1/n\\) throughout the low band. Thus the positively labelled vertices are exactly \\(U\\), including against every history-dependent opponent.</p><p>Proposition 6 bounds the complete uniform deterministic computation by\n\\[\n2^{C(\\log_2(L+2))^2}\n\\]\nbit operations for an absolute constant \\(C\\). The transformations, game reduction, and bit accounting all allow signed binary weights, self-loops, and separately listed parallel edges.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 7.1, “Full game solution.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the game, strategy, payoff, and input conventions of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{val}_w(v)\n  =\n  \\sup_\\sigma\\inf_\\tau\n  \\operatorname{MP}_w(\\pi_{v,\\sigma,\\tau}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A positional strategy chooses an outgoing edge using only the current vertex.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A uniform deterministic algorithm returns the exact value \\(\\operatorname{val}_w(v)\\), as a reduced rational, for every \\(v\\in V\\), and one positional strategy \\(\\sigma^\\star\\) for Max and one positional strategy \\(\\tau^\\star\\) for Min such that, simultaneously for every \\(v\\in V\\) and all history-dependent strategies \\(\\sigma,\\tau\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{MP}_w(\\pi_{v,\\sigma^\\star,\\tau})\n\\ge \\operatorname{val}_w(v),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{MP}_w(\\pi_{v,\\sigma,\\tau^\\star})\n\\le \\operatorname{val}_w(v).\n\\]\nThe complete computation takes\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Theorem 1 to implement the correct zero-threshold winning-set interface required by Proposition 7. Proposition 7 reconstructs every exact value by threshold queries and rational reconstruction. Proposition 8 then recovers the two positional strategies by separate value-preserving self-reductions, each beginning with the original game. They have the simultaneous guarantees against arbitrary history-dependent opponents.</p><p>The combined reduction makes at most\n\\[\nQ=nK(1+m)\n\\]\nthreshold calls, polynomially many in \\(L+1\\). Every queried game has polynomial binary length in \\(L+1\\). Applying Theorem 1 to these games and multiplying by \\(Q\\) gives\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations. The reconstruction, comparisons, edge-list copying, and polynomial-size value and strategy output are included.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 7.2, “Randomized full game solution.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the game, value, positional-strategy, and input conventions of Theorems 1 and 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the companion article’s winning-set algorithm: it returns the whole zero-threshold winning set correctly with probability at least \\(7/8\\), and runs in quasipolynomial bit time on every random tape.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A uniform randomized algorithm returns all exact values and both globally optimal positional strategies specified in Theorem 2 with probability at least \\(7/8\\) for the entire output. Its running time is\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations on every random tape.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the value and strategy reduction of Theorem 2, as specified in Propositions 7 and 8, using the companion’s randomized winning-set algorithm [24, Theorem 1.1]. Its whole-set success probability is at least \\(7/8\\), and its quasipolynomial running-time bound holds on every tape.</p><p>The number of threshold queries is bounded by\n\\[\nQ=nK(1+m).\n\\]\nFor each query, take\n\\[\nr=2\\lceil\\log_2(8Q)\\rceil+1\n\\]\nindependent fresh runs and use the coordinatewise majority of their winning sets.</p><p>Conditional on the preceding history, the current query is fixed. The amplified set is correct whenever more than half the runs return the whole correct set. Its conditional failure probability is at most\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{j=\\lceil r/2\\rceil}^{r}\n\\binom rj(1/8)^j\n&amp;\\le 2^r(1/8)^{r/2}\\\\\n&amp;=2^{-r/2}\n&lt;\\frac1{8Q}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nA first-error union bound over adaptive queries makes every threshold answer correct with probability at least \\(7/8\\). On that event, the deterministic reduction yields all exact values and both globally optimal positional strategies.</p><p>On other tapes, if rational reconstruction does not yield a unique rational or no value-preserving trial is found, stop with zero at every value coordinate and the first listed outgoing edge at every owned vertex. All loops retain their stated caps.</p><p>Since \\(r=O(\\log(L+2))\\), and queried encodings have polynomial length on every tape, the total bound remains\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\non every tape, including random-bit generation. Only the winning-set interface of the companion is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary 8.1, “Fixed and minimum energy credits.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the finite total game with signed binary integer weights from Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For a start \\(v\\in V\\) and a nonnegative integer credit \\(c\\), call \\((v,c)\\) energy-winning if Max has a strategy such that, against every history-dependent Min strategy,\n  \\[\n  c+\\sum_{t=0}^{j-1}w(e_t)\\ge 0\n  \\qquad\\text{for every }j\\ge 0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">There is one accumulated resource and no upper bound or saturation.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c_{\\min}(v)\\) be the least nonnegative integer winning credit, and put \\(c_{\\min}(v)=\\infty\\) if none exists.</p><p class=\"li\">A positional Max policy specifies one labelled outgoing edge at each Max vertex.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L_c\\) be the full explicit input length including the supplied start and credit; let \\(L\\) be the original game input length.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniform deterministic algorithms have the following guarantees.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Given \\(v\\) and a nonnegative binary integer \\(c\\), decide whether \\((v,c)\\) is energy-winning. On a positive answer, return a positional Max policy satisfying the energy inequalities from that start and credit against every history-dependent Min strategy.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Given \\(v\\), return the exact \\(c_{\\min}(v)\\), including the possible answer \\(\\infty\\). On a finite answer, return a positional Max policy winning from \\(v\\) at credit \\(c_{\\min}(v)\\).</p><p>The fixed-credit computation takes\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L_c+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations. The minimum-credit computation takes\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations. Handling every vertex by separate per-start searches, including a separate policy for every finite answer, has the same form of total bit bound.</p><p>Each policy may depend on the queried start and credit; it also wins from that same start at every larger credit.</p>", "proof": "<p>For prescribed \\((v,c)\\), construct \\(R_{v,c}\\) as in Proposition 9. By that proposition,\n\\[\n(v,c)\\text{ is energy-winning}\n\\iff\nv^{\\mathrm{in}}\\in U(R_{v,c}).\n\\]\nTheorem 1 decides this membership. On a positive answer, apply Theorem 2 to the reset game and restrict its optimal Max strategy to the original Max exits. Proposition 9 proves that this is the requested positional energy policy.</p><p>For minimum credit, put\n\\[\nW_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\max_e|w(e)|,\\qquad\nH=(n-1)W_{\\mathrm{in}}.\n\\]\nCompute \\(U(G)\\) once by Theorem 1. Proposition 10 proves that starts outside \\(U(G)\\) have minimum credit \\(\\infty\\), while every start in \\(U(G)\\) has minimum credit in \\([0,H]\\).</p><p>For a winning start, the fixed-credit predicate is monotone: a strategy winning at credit \\(c\\) also wins at every larger credit. Binary search with formal losing lower endpoint \\(\\ell=-1\\) and known winning upper endpoint \\(h=H\\). While \\(h-\\ell&gt;1\\), query\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\ell+h}{2}\\right\\rfloor\n\\]\nand replace the endpoint with the same answer. The sentinel is never queried. At most\n\\[\n\\lceil\\log_2(H+1)\\rceil\n\\]\nfixed-credit decisions return the exact minimum. If \\(H=0\\), the initial gap is one and no query is made. To return a policy at the minimum, apply Theorem 2 to \\(R_{v,c_{\\min}(v)}\\) and restrict its Max choices.</p><p>Let \\(b_c=O(1+\\log_2(c+1))\\) be the supplied credit’s bit length. The reset game has encoding length\n\\[\nO\\bigl(L+n(b_c+\\log_2(n+1))\\bigr),\n\\]\npolynomial in \\(L_c\\). The credit is copied into only \\(n\\) edge records; no array indexed by its numerical value is constructed. Theorems 1 and 2 therefore give the stated fixed-credit bound.</p><p>For minimum credits, \\(H\\) and every queried credit have\n\\[\nO(1+\\log_2(n+1)+\\log_2(W_{\\mathrm{in}}+1))\n=O(L+1)\n\\]\nbits. All queried games have polynomial encoding length in \\(L+1\\).</p><p>For all vertices, the outer reduction makes at most\n\\[\n1+n\\lceil\\log_2(H+1)\\rceil\n\\]\ndirect winning-set calls, including the original one, and at most one full-solution call for every requested finite-credit policy. These counts are polynomial. Polynomially many calls on polynomial-length inputs preserve\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit complexity. Computing \\(H\\), binary-search arithmetic, copying policies, and the polynomial-length output are included. Each returned policy also wins from its queried start at every larger credit.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "Corollary 9.1, “Tropical feasibility and fixed corank.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A,B\\) be explicitly listed \\(m\\times n\\) matrices and \\(c,d\\) be \\(m\\)-dimensional vectors over \\(\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{-\\infty\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(a+(-\\infty)=-\\infty\\) and \\(\\max\\varnothing=-\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Variables range over \\(\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be the complete explicit binary input length.</p><p class=\"li\">The finite-coordinate support of a vector \\(x\\) is \\(\\{j:x_j\\ne-\\infty\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Tropical rank is the largest order of a square submatrix whose permutation sums have a finite, unique maximum. It is zero if no such nonempty submatrix exists.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Feasibility of\n\\[\n\\max\\left\\{c_i,\\max_j(A_{ij}+x_j)\\right\\}\n\\le\n\\max\\left\\{d_i,\\max_j(B_{ij}+x_j)\\right\\}\n\\qquad\\text{for every row }i\n\\]\nis decidable deterministically in\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations. Feasibility with all coordinates finite is decidable within the same bound. Equalities between the displayed maxima are allowed, interpreted as pairs of inequalities.</p><p>For a homogeneous system, \\(c=d=-\\infty\\), one can also compute the maximal finite-coordinate support of a solution, including the empty support. In particular, the same bound decides feasibility of max-atom systems\n\\[\nz\\le \\max(x,y)+a\n\\]\nwith rational constants \\(a\\) and finite real, equivalently rational, variables.</p><p>For every fixed integer \\(k\\ge 0\\), a deterministic algorithm with bit bound\n\\[\n2^{O_k((\\log(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\ndecides whether an explicitly listed \\(m\\times n\\) matrix over \\(\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{-\\infty\\}\\), with \\(m\\ge n\\ge 1\\), has tropical rank at least \\(n-k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Clear rational denominators by the polynomial-bit scaling in Proposition 11.</p><p>For a homogeneous system, Proposition 11 reduces maximal finite-coordinate support to the winning columns of a total signed-integer-weight mean-payoff game of polynomial encoding length. Theorem 1 computes that winning set in\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations. Forced-\\(-\\infty\\) coordinates are excluded, and the empty support is covered.</p><p>Proposition 12 reduces affine feasibility to whether the homogenizing coordinate belongs to the maximal support, and all-finite feasibility to whether all \\(n+1\\) coordinates belong. It also handles equalities and max-atom systems, including equivalence of finite real and rational solutions. These transformations have polynomial encoding size, giving the first part of the theorem.</p><p>For fixed \\(k\\), Proposition 13 handles \\(n\\le k\\) directly. Otherwise it reduces tropical rank at least \\(n-k\\) to at most \\(n^k\\) homogeneous-support tests, each of polynomial encoding length. Applying the preceding support algorithm to each test gives total bit cost\n\\[\n2^{O_k((\\log(L+2))^2)}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, “Finite-box comparison and basic iteration”; its hypotheses use equations (2.1)–(2.4)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite total game on \\(V=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}\\), with ownership partition \\(V_{\\mathrm{Max}},V_{\\mathrm{Min}}\\) and signed integer weights \\(w\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\widehat w(e)=(n+1)w(e)+1,\n  \\qquad\n  W=\\max_{e\\in E}|\\widehat w(e)|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(z\\in\\mathbb Z^V\\), define\n  \\[\n  F_i(z)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  \\displaystyle\\max_{e:i\\to j}\\bigl(\\widehat w(e)+z_j\\bigr),\n     &amp;i\\in V_{\\mathrm{Max}},\\\\{}[1ex]\n  \\displaystyle\\min_{e:i\\to j}\\bigl(\\widehat w(e)+z_j\\bigr),\n     &amp;i\\in V_{\\mathrm{Min}}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">All vector inequalities, maxima, and minima are coordinatewise. A scalar added to a vector is added at every coordinate.</p><p class=\"li\">For integer vectors \\(A\\le u\\), let \\([A,u]\\) be the set of integer vectors between them.</p><p class=\"li\">A subsolution is \\(x\\in[A,u]\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  x_i\\le F_i(x)\\quad\\text{wherever }x_i&gt;A_i.\n  \\]\n  A supersolution is \\(y\\in[A,u]\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  y_i\\ge F_i(y)\\quad\\text{wherever }y_i&lt;u_i.\n  \\]\n  No inequality is required on the respective excluded boundary.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  T_{A,u}(z)=\\min\\{u,\\max\\{A,F(z)\\}\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every subsolution \\(x\\) and supersolution \\(y\\) in \\([A,u]\\) satisfy \\(x\\le y\\). There is a unique \\(h\\in[A,u]\\) that is both a subsolution and a supersolution. It is precisely the unique fixed point of \\(T_{A,u}\\), and every such pair satisfies\n\\[\nx\\le h\\le y.\n\\]</p><p>The procedure \\(\\mathrm{Basic}\\) starts with \\(p=A\\), evaluates \\(q=T_{A,u}(p)\\), returns \\(p\\) if \\(q=p\\), and otherwise sets \\(p=q\\) and repeats. It finds \\(h\\) using at most\n\\[\n1+\\sum_{i\\in V}(u_i-A_i)\n\\]\nevaluations, including the final stability test.</p><p>The statement permits negative endpoints and collapsed coordinates \\(A_i=u_i\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The edge set is nonempty. Every transformed weight is a nonzero integer, so \\(W\\ge 1\\). If a simple directed cycle has length \\(\\ell\\) and original sum \\(c\\), then\n\\[\n\\widehat w(C)=(n+1)c+\\ell,\n\\qquad 1\\le\\ell\\le n.\n\\]\nFor \\(c\\ge 0\\), this sum is positive; for \\(c\\le-1\\), it is negative. Thus no simple cycle has zero transformed sum, and positive transformed simple-cycle sums correspond exactly to nonnegative original sums. The calculation applies to self-loops and to actual edge sequences with parallel edges.</p><p>The finite, nonempty outgoing-edge sets ensure that the extrema defining \\(F\\) are attained. Moreover,\n\\[\nz\\le z'\\Longrightarrow F(z)\\le F(z'),\n\\qquad\nF(z+t)=F(z)+t\n\\quad(t\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\]</p><p>The comparison, unique fixed-point, and iteration assertions are imported from the complete September 25 companion article, Definition 2.2 and Lemmas 2.3–2.4 [24]. The operator and integer hypotheses are exactly those above, and the cycle calculation verifies the required absence of zero simple-cycle sums. The comparison conclusion concerns all vertices, even though each hypothesis is imposed only on its stated support. Comparing with \\(h\\) on each side gives the sandwich inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 2.2, “Winning partition from the wide box.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite total game on \\(n\\ge 1\\) vertices, with ownership partition and signed integer weights \\(w\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\widehat w(e)=(n+1)w(e)+1,\n  \\qquad W=\\max_e|\\widehat w(e)|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F_i(z)\\) be the maximum of \\(\\widehat w(e)+z_j\\) over outgoing edges \\(e:i\\to j\\) at Max vertices, and the minimum at Min vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose the dyadic width\n  \\[\n  D_0=\\min\\left\\{\n  2^d:d\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0},\\\n  2^d\\ge64(n+1)(W+1)\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(h\\) be the unique fixed point of\n  \\[\n  z\\longmapsto\\min\\{D_0,\\max\\{0,F(z)\\}\\}\n  \\]\n  in \\([0,D_0]^V\\), supplied by Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  V_{\\mathrm{lo}}=\\{i:h_i\\le nW\\},\n  \\qquad\n  V_{\\mathrm{hi}}=\\{i:h_i\\ge D_0-nW\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The sets \\(V_{\\mathrm{lo}}\\) and \\(V_{\\mathrm{hi}}\\) partition \\(V\\).</p><p>There is a positional Max strategy under which every play starting in \\(V_{\\mathrm{hi}}\\) has original mean payoff at least zero. There is a positional Min strategy under which every play starting in \\(V_{\\mathrm{lo}}\\) has original upper limiting average at most \\(-1/n\\). Both assertions allow arbitrary history-dependent opposition.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(0&lt;h_i&lt;D_0\\), both box inequalities apply and give \\(h_i=F_i(h)\\). Follow an edge attaining that equality and continue from its head as long as the current coordinate is interior. A first repeated vertex would yield a simple cycle of tight equalities\n\\[\nh_i=\\widehat w(e)+h_j\n\\]\nwith transformed sum zero, contrary to the cycle calculation in Proposition 1. A boundary is therefore reached within \\(n\\) edges. Each edge changes the potential by at most \\(W\\). Including the length-zero path from a boundary coordinate, every coordinate is within \\(nW\\) of either \\(0\\) or \\(D_0\\). Since \\(D_0&gt;2nW\\), the two bands are disjoint and exhaust \\(V\\).</p><p>Every high coordinate is above the lower boundary. At a high Max vertex choose an outgoing edge satisfying\n\\[\nh_i\\le\\widehat w(e)+h_j;\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nsuch an edge exists by the subsolution inequality. At a high Min vertex every outgoing edge satisfies this inequality. Every permitted edge has\n\\[\nh_j\\ge D_0-(n+1)W&gt;nW,\n\\]\nso its head is high. Fix the Max choices and extend them arbitrarily outside the high band.</p><p>On a simple cycle of permitted edges, inequality (2.6) telescopes to a nonnegative transformed sum. Its original sum is therefore nonnegative. Put \\(W_0=\\max_e|w(e)|\\). Delete simple cycles successively from any finite play prefix until fewer than \\(n\\) edges remain. The removed cycles have nonnegative original sums, and the remaining walk has weight at least \\(-(n-1)W_0\\). Thus every prefix of every consistent play has at least that weight. Dividing by its length and taking a lower limit proves Max’s assertion.</p><p>Every low coordinate is below the upper boundary. At a low Min vertex fix an edge attaining the minimum in \\(F_i(h)\\), and at a low Max vertex permit all outgoing edges. The supersolution inequality gives\n\\[\nh_i\\ge\\widehat w(e)+h_j\n\\tag{2.7}\n\\]\nfor every permitted edge. Its head satisfies\n\\[\nh_j\\le(n+1)W&lt;D_0-nW,\n\\]\nso it is low. Extend these choices arbitrarily elsewhere.</p><p>Every simple cycle of permitted edges has nonpositive transformed sum and hence original integer sum at most \\(-1\\). Deleting simple cycles from a prefix of length \\(T\\) leaves at most \\(n-1\\) edges. Each deleted cycle has at most \\(n\\) edges, so the number removed is at least\n\\[\n\\frac{T-(n-1)}n.\n\\]\nThe original prefix weight is at most\n\\[\n-\\frac{T-(n-1)}n+(n-1)W_0.\n\\]\nIts upper limiting average is at most \\(-1/n\\). Both prefix estimates apply to every consistent edge sequence, independently of how the opposing player uses the history.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, “Translated supports and claims”; witness terminology is Definition 2.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the operator \\(F:\\mathbb Z^V\\to\\mathbb Z^V\\) of Proposition 1. In particular, it is monotone and satisfies \\(F(z+t)=F(z)+t\\) for integer scalar shifts.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  A\\in\\mathbb Z^V,\\qquad D=2^d\\ge4096,\\qquad\n  a,b\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}^V.\n  \\]\n  Put \\(u=A+D\\) and \\(P=D/32\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(S\\subseteq V\\), define \\(a(S)=\\sum_{i\\in S}a_i\\) and \\(b(S)=\\sum_{i\\in S}b_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A Max witness is a vector \\(x\\in[A,u]\\) satisfying \\(x_i\\le F_i(x)\\) whenever \\(x_i&gt;A_i\\), whose support\n  \\[\n  S_x=\\{i:x_i&gt;A_i\\}\n  \\]\n  has \\(a(S_x)\\le1\\). It claims each \\(i\\) with \\(x_i\\ge u_i-P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A Min witness is a vector \\(y\\in[A,u]\\) satisfying \\(y_i\\ge F_i(y)\\) whenever \\(y_i&lt;u_i\\), whose support\n  \\[\n  S_y=\\{i:y_i&lt;u_i\\}\n  \\]\n  has \\(b(S_y)\\le1\\). It claims each \\(i\\) with \\(y_i\\le A_i+P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A witness is relevant if it claims at least one index.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  c=A+D/2,\\qquad M=D/8,\\qquad\n  q=D/128,\\qquad p=q/32=D/4096.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be an integer pivot satisfying \\(c-M\\le k\\le c+M\\), and let \\(r\\in\\{q,D/2\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a relevant Max witness \\(x\\), define\n  \\[\n  s_x(k)=\\max_i(x_i-k_i),\\qquad\n  g_i^x(k)=s_x(k)-(x_i-k_i).\n  \\]\n  For a relevant Min witness \\(y\\), define\n  \\[\n  s_y(k)=\\max_i(k_i-y_i),\\qquad\n  g_i^y(k)=s_y(k)-(k_i-y_i).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every relevant Max witness \\(x\\), the vector\n\\[\nx'_i=\\max\\{k_i-r/2,\\ x_i-s_x(k)+r/2\\}\n\\]\nis an integer subsolution in \\([k-r/2,k+r/2]\\), with support\n\\[\nS_{x'}=\\{i:g_i^x(k)&lt;r\\}\\subseteq S_x.\n\\]\nFor any positive new Max masses \\(a'\\) with \\(a'(S_{x'})\\le1\\), it is a Max witness and claims every \\(i\\) with \\(g_i^x(k)\\le r/32\\).</p><p>For every relevant Min witness \\(y\\), the vector\n\\[\ny'_i=\\min\\{k_i+r/2,\\ y_i+s_y(k)-r/2\\}\n\\]\nis an integer supersolution in the same box, with support\n\\[\nS_{y'}=\\{i:g_i^y(k)&lt;r\\}\\subseteq S_y.\n\\]\nFor any positive new Min masses \\(b'\\) with \\(b'(S_{y'})\\le1\\), it is a Min witness and claims every \\(i\\) with \\(g_i^y(k)\\le r/32\\).</p><p>In particular, unchanged masses \\(a,b\\) always make the corresponding translated vectors witnesses.</p>", "proof": "<p>For Max, relevance supplies a claim at some \\(j\\), so\n\\[\nx_j\\ge A_j+D-P,\n\\qquad\nk_j\\le A_j+D/2+M,\n\\]\nand therefore\n\\[\ns_x(k)\\ge D/2-M-P.\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nIf \\(i\\notin S_x\\), then \\(x_i=A_i\\) and \\(x_i-k_i\\le-D/2+M\\). Consequently,\n\\[\ng_i^x(k)\\ge D-2M-P=\\frac{23D}{32}&gt;\\frac D2.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]</p><p>The untruncated term defining \\(x'_i\\) equals\n\\[\nk_i+r/2-g_i^x(k).\n\\]\nIt lies below or at the new upper boundary. Taking its maximum with the lower boundary puts \\(x'\\) in the new box, with support exactly where \\(g_i^x(k)&lt;r\\). Since \\(r\\le D/2\\), inequality (3.7) proves support containment. The half-widths and peaks are integral.</p><p>Set \\(t=-s_x(k)+r/2\\). Globally \\(x'\\ge x+t\\), while on the new support \\(x'_i=x_i+t\\) and the parent’s subsolution inequality applies. Hence\n\\[\nF_i(x')\n\\ge F_i(x+t)\n=F_i(x)+t\n\\ge x_i+t\n=x'_i.\n\\]\nThus \\(x'\\) is a subsolution. The witness condition is precisely the stated mass bound. If \\(g_i^x(k)\\le r/32\\), its untruncated entry is at least\n\\[\nk_i+r/2-r/32,\n\\]\nthe new Max claim threshold; taking a maximum with the lower boundary cannot destroy that claim.</p><p>For Min, relevance similarly gives \\(s_y(k)\\ge D/2-M-P\\). Outside \\(S_y\\), one has \\(y_i=A_i+D\\), so \\(k_i-y_i\\le-D/2+M\\). The gap is again at least \\(23D/32&gt;D/2\\). The translated term before clipping equals\n\\[\nk_i-r/2+g_i^y(k).\n\\]\nIt is above or at the lower boundary. Taking its minimum with the upper boundary leaves support exactly at gaps \\(&lt;r\\), contained in \\(S_y\\).</p><p>Set \\(t=s_y(k)-r/2\\). The inequality \\(y'\\le y+t\\), with equality on the new support, gives\n\\[\nF_i(y')\n\\le F_i(y+t)\n=F_i(y)+t\n\\le y_i+t\n=y'_i.\n\\]\nThis is the supersolution inequality. The mass condition makes \\(y'\\) a witness, and a gap at most \\(r/32\\) gives the asserted Min claim.</p><p>Finally, support containment and positivity imply\n\\[\na(S_{x'})\\le a(S_x)\\le1,\n\\qquad\nb(S_{y'})\\le b(S_y)\\le1.\n\\]\nThe strict support threshold \\(g&lt;r\\) and the non-strict claim threshold \\(g\\le r/32\\) have the distinct roles stated.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Procedure 4.1 and Lemmas 4.2–4.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite total game and the transformed max/min operator \\(F\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Inputs have the form\n  \\[\n  A\\in\\mathbb Z^V,\\qquad\n  D=2^d\\ (d\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}),\\qquad\n  a,b\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}^V.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The input box is \\([A,A+D]\\). The procedure \\(\\mathrm{Basic}\\) is the finite iteration specified in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">A centered width-\\(r\\) call at an integer pivot \\(k\\), where \\(r\\) is even, means\n  \\[\n  \\mathrm{Label}(k-r/2,r,a',b')\n  \\]\n  on \\([k-r/2,k+r/2]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(\\mathrm{Label}(A,D,a,b)\\) by the following steps, executing the first applicable base case.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. **Mass base case.** If \\(a_i b_i&gt;1\\) for every \\(i\\), return \\(+\\) where \\(b_i&gt;1\\), and return \\(-\\) elsewhere.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. **Width base case.** If \\(D&lt;4096\\), compute the box solution \\(z\\) by \\(\\mathrm{Basic}\\). Return \\(+\\) at \\(i\\) if\n   \\[\n   z_i&gt;A_i+D/2,\n   \\]\n   and return \\(-\\) otherwise.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. **Downward pass.** Set\n   \\[\n   P=D/32,\\quad c=A+D/2,\\quad M=D/8,\\quad\n   q=D/128,\\quad p=D/4096.\n   \\]\n   Initialize \\(k=c\\). Repeatedly make a centered width-\\(q\\) call with masses \\((a,\\frac43b)\\), and use its labels to set simultaneously\n   \\[\n   k_i^{\\mathrm{new}}=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\max\\{c_i-M,k_i-p\\},&amp;\\text{if the label at }i\\text{ is }-,\\\\\n   k_i,&amp;\\text{if the label at }i\\text{ is }+.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Replace \\(k\\) by \\(k^{\\mathrm{new}}\\). Stop when the update leaves \\(k\\) unchanged. Denote this pivot by \\(k^\\downarrow\\), and record\n   \\[\n   I_-=\\{i:k_i^\\downarrow&lt;c_i-P\\}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">4. **Upward pass.** Start from \\(k=k^\\downarrow\\). Repeatedly make a centered width-\\(q\\) call with masses \\((\\frac43a,b)\\), and set simultaneously\n   \\[\n   k_i^{\\mathrm{new}}=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\min\\{c_i+M,k_i+p\\},&amp;\\text{if the label at }i\\text{ is }+,\\\\\n   k_i,&amp;\\text{if the label at }i\\text{ is }-.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Stop at an unchanged update. Write \\(k^f\\) for the final pivot, and record\n   \\[\n   I_+=\\{i:k_i^f&gt;c_i+P\\}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">5. **Provisional labels and masses.** Make one centered width-\\(D/2\\) call at \\(k^f\\) with masses \\((a,b)\\). For its labels \\(\\lambda^0\\), put\n   \\[\n   (\\bar a_i,\\bar b_i)=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   (\\frac45a_i,\\frac85b_i),&amp;\\lambda_i^0=+,\\\\\n   (\\frac85a_i,\\frac45b_i),&amp;\\lambda_i^0=-.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">6. **Continuation and overrides.** Call\n   \\[\n   \\mathrm{Label}(A,D,\\bar a,\\bar b).\n   \\]\n   In its output put \\(-\\) on \\(I_-\\) and \\(+\\) on \\(I_+\\setminus I_-\\), leaving all other labels unchanged.</p><p class=\"li\">For the analysis only, define\n  \\[\n  \\mu(a,b)=\\min_i a_i b_i,\\qquad\n  \\rho=\\frac{32}{25},\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  B(\\mu)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  0,&amp;\\mu&gt;1,\\\\\n  1+\\lfloor\\log_\\rho(1/\\mu)\\rfloor,&amp;0&lt;\\mu\\le1.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The procedure terminates on every input. Every recursive child is valid and its box is contained in its parent’s box. Each pivot pass uses at most \\(1024n+1\\) child calls.</p><p>Every child of a nonbase call has smaller nonnegative rank\n\\[\nR=d+B(\\mu(a,b)).\n\\]\nThe provisional child in Step 5 is the only child preserving \\(B\\); it halves the width. Every other child decreases \\(B\\) by at least one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 proves validity, nesting, pivot bounds, and the bounded number of pass calls whenever children terminate. Lemma 4.2 supplies the strictly decreasing rank and proves termination by induction. Together they establish all assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the input, procedure, and parameters of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider a nonbase call.</p><p class=\"li\">For the pass-count assertion, assume its child calls terminate.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every child is a valid input and its box is contained in its parent’s box. The pivot satisfies\n\\[\nc-M\\le k\\le c\n\\quad\\text{during the downward pass},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nc-M\\le k\\le c+M\n\\quad\\text{during the upward pass}.\n\\]\nEach pass makes at most \\(1024n+1\\) calls.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(D\\ge4096\\) is dyadic, all parameters, both child half-widths, and all pivot coordinates are integral. The child widths \\(D/128\\) and \\(D/2\\) are positive powers of two. The update caps imply the pivot bounds.</p><p>A centered child has width \\(r\\le D/2\\), and its endpoints satisfy\n\\[\nc-M-r/2\\ge A+D/8\\ge A,\n\\qquad\nc+M+r/2\\le A+7D/8\\le A+D.\n\\]\nThe continuation has the unchanged parent box. All mass multipliers preserve positive rationality.</p><p>The pivot lies on the grid of spacing \\(p\\) through \\(c\\), since \\(M/p=512\\) is integral and both caps belong to that grid. In a changing update, at least one coordinate moves by at least \\(p\\). Each pass is coordinatewise monotone over a range of at most \\(2M\\), so a coordinate moves at most\n\\[\n2M/p=1024\n\\]\ntimes. At most \\(1024n\\) updates can change the pivot. One further child call certifies the unchanged update at which the pass stops.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.3, “Well-founded recursion.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Inherit the procedure, \\(\\mu,\\rho,B\\), and rank \\(R=d+B(\\mu)\\) of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every recursive child of a nonbase call has smaller rank \\(R\\). The provisional child is the only child preserving \\(B\\); all other children decrease \\(B\\) by at least one. The procedure terminates on every valid input.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(\\mu&gt;1\\), the mass base case applies. Otherwise, multiplying \\(\\mu\\) by a factor at least \\(\\rho\\) decreases \\(B\\) by at least one: either the new value exceeds one, or its logarithm falls by at least one before rounding down.</p><p>In either pivot pass, every product \\(a_i b_i\\) is multiplied by \\(4/3\\ge\\rho\\). In the continuation, every product is multiplied by\n\\[\n(4/5)(8/5)=\\rho,\n\\]\nindependently of the provisional label. These children decrease \\(B\\) and do not increase \\(d\\). The provisional child keeps the masses and halves the width, decreasing \\(d\\) by one. Every child therefore has strictly smaller nonnegative rank.</p><p>Induct on \\(R\\). Base cases require finite arithmetic and, in the width case, the finite iteration of Proposition 1. At a nonbase node every child terminates by induction. Lemma 4.1 then bounds both passes; the remaining two children and all local operations are finite. No correctness assumption about the labels is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Theorem 2.4, “Simultaneous labelling.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the transformed operator \\(F\\) of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  A\\in\\mathbb Z^V,\\qquad D=2^d,\\ d\\ge0,\\qquad\n  a,b\\in\\mathbb Q_{&gt;0}^V.\n  \\]\n  Put \\(u=A+D\\) and \\(P=D/32\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(S\\subseteq V\\), use \\(a(S)=\\sum_{i\\in S}a_i\\) and \\(b(S)=\\sum_{i\\in S}b_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A Max witness is a subsolution \\(x\\in[A,u]\\), meaning \\(x_i\\le F_i(x)\\) wherever \\(x_i&gt;A_i\\), with\n  \\[\n  S_x=\\{i:x_i&gt;A_i\\},\\qquad a(S_x)\\le1.\n  \\]\n  It claims every \\(i\\) with \\(x_i\\ge u_i-P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A Min witness is a supersolution \\(y\\in[A,u]\\), meaning \\(y_i\\ge F_i(y)\\) wherever \\(y_i&lt;u_i\\), with\n  \\[\n  S_y=\\{i:y_i&lt;u_i\\},\\qquad b(S_y)\\le1.\n  \\]\n  It claims every \\(i\\) with \\(y_i\\le A_i+P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A relevant witness has at least one claim.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathrm{Label}\\) be the procedure of Proposition 4. At a nonbase input use its parameters \\(c,M,q,p\\), pivots \\(k^\\downarrow,k^f\\), marks \\(I_-,I_+\\), provisional labels \\(\\lambda^0\\), and masses \\(\\bar a,\\bar b\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For relevant witnesses, define\n  \\[\n  s_x(k)=\\max_i(x_i-k_i),\\quad\n  g_i^x(k)=s_x(k)-(x_i-k_i),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  s_y(k)=\\max_i(k_i-y_i),\\quad\n  g_i^y(k)=s_y(k)-(k_i-y_i).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every valid input, \\(\\mathrm{Label}(A,D,a,b)\\) returns a vector in \\(\\{+,-\\}^V\\) assigning \\(+\\) to every index claimed by any Max witness and \\(-\\) to every index claimed by any Min witness.</p><p>All witnesses are covered by the same output. They are neither enumerated nor supplied to the procedure. Vertices with no claim may receive either label.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(P&lt;D\\), every claim belongs to its witness’s support. Opposite claims cannot coincide: Proposition 1 would give\n\\[\nu_i-P\\le x_i\\le y_i\\le A_i+P,\n\\]\ncontradicting \\(2P&lt;D\\).</p><p>Use induction on the rank \\(R=d+B(\\mu)\\) from Proposition 4, which already establishes termination.</p><p>In the mass base case, \\(a_i b_i&gt;1\\) at every vertex. If \\(b_i&gt;1\\), no Min witness can contain \\(i\\) in its support and hence no Min witness can claim it; returning \\(+\\) is permitted. If \\(b_i\\le1\\), then \\(a_i&gt;1\\), so no Max witness can claim \\(i\\); returning \\(-\\) is permitted.</p><p>In the width base case, Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\nx\\le z\\le y\n\\]\nfor every Max witness \\(x\\), every Min witness \\(y\\), and the computed box solution \\(z\\). At a Max claim,\n\\[\nz_i\\ge A_i+D-P&gt;A_i+D/2,\n\\]\nso the procedure returns \\(+\\). At a Min claim,\n\\[\nz_i\\le A_i+P&lt;A_i+D/2,\n\\]\nso it returns \\(-\\). This also applies when \\(D=1\\), where the midpoint is a rational threshold.</p><p>In a nonbase call, all children have smaller rank and satisfy the simultaneous guarantee by induction. Lemmas 5.1–5.3 apply.</p><p>Fix a Max witness and an index \\(i\\) it claims. Lemma 5.1 excludes \\(i\\) from \\(I_-\\). If \\(i\\in I_+\\), the positive override settles it. Otherwise the witness has a claim outside \\(I_+\\) and survives reweighting by Lemma 5.3. The continuation labels \\(i\\) positively, and no override changes it.</p><p>For a Min claim, Lemma 5.2 excludes \\(I_+\\). A claim in \\(I_-\\) receives the negative override. A claim outside \\(I_-\\) belongs to a surviving Min witness, receives \\(-\\) from the continuation, and is not changed afterward.</p><p>Although \\(I_-\\) refers to the earlier pivot, its safety was proved for fixed parent witnesses and remains valid after the upward pass. The marks may intersect, but their intersection contains no claim of either type; negative precedence causes no conflict. Every vertex receives exactly one label, and the witnesses and claimed indices were arbitrary. Thus all obligations hold simultaneously.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, “The downward pass.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit a nonbase input and all notation from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume every recursive child satisfies the simultaneous labelling guarantee: it labels every Max claim \\(+\\) and every Min claim \\(-\\) for its own box and masses.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every relevant Max witness \\(x\\), the peak \\(s_x(k)\\) is constant throughout the downward pass and is at most \\(D/2\\). No Max witness claims an index in \\(I_-\\).</p><p>For every Min witness \\(y\\) having a claim outside \\(I_-\\),\n\\[\nb\\bigl(\\{i:g_i^y(k^\\downarrow)&lt;q\\}\\bigr)&gt;\\frac34.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(x\\) and one downward update from \\(k\\). Its width-\\(q\\) translation is a witness for the child, since the child keeps Max masses \\(a\\). By Proposition 3, every coordinate with gap at most \\(q/32=p\\) is a Max claim of the translated witness. The child labels these coordinates \\(+\\), so they do not move. At least one gap-zero coordinate continues to attain the old peak.</p><p>Every other coordinate moves downward by some \\(\\delta_i\\in[0,p]\\). Its gap is greater than \\(p\\), and\n\\[\nx_i-k_i^{\\mathrm{new}}\n=\ns_x(k)-g_i^x(k)+\\delta_i\n&lt;\ns_x(k).\n\\]\nNo coordinate overtakes the old peak, even though many may move simultaneously. The peak is unchanged. Initially \\(k=c\\) and \\(x\\le A+D\\), so \\(s_x(c)\\le D/2\\). If \\(x\\) claims \\(i\\), then\n\\[\nk_i^\\downarrow\n\\ge x_i-D/2\n\\ge A_i+D/2-P\n=c_i-P,\n\\]\nexcluding \\(i\\) from \\(I_-\\).</p><p>For the mass assertion, suppose \\(y\\) claims \\(j\\notin I_-\\). Then\n\\[\nk_j^\\downarrow\\ge c_j-P,\\qquad y_j\\le A_j+P,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\ns_y(k^\\downarrow)\\ge D/2-2P&gt;D/2-M.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nAt a lower-capped coordinate,\n\\[\nk_i^\\downarrow-y_i\\le D/2-M\n\\]\nbecause \\(y_i\\ge A_i\\). Every maximizer is therefore strictly above its cap.</p><p>If the asserted mass were at most \\(3/4\\), the width-\\(q\\) Min translation, whose support is exactly the gaps \\(&lt;q\\), would have support mass at most one under the last child’s masses \\(\\frac43b\\). Its gap-zero maximizers would receive \\(-\\). At least one would move downward by a positive amount, since it is above its cap and \\(p&gt;0\\). This contradicts the final unchanged update. Equality at \\(3/4\\) gives the same contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2, “The upward pass.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the nonbase input and notation of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume all recursive children satisfy the simultaneous labelling guarantee, as in Lemma 5.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every relevant Min witness \\(y\\), the peak \\(s_y(k)\\) is constant throughout the upward pass and is at most \\(D/2\\). No Min witness claims an index in \\(I_+\\).</p><p>At the final pivot,\n\\[\nb\\bigl(\\{i:g_i^y(k^f)&lt;q\\}\\bigr)&gt;\\frac34\n\\quad\\text{if }y\\text{ has a claim outside }I_-,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\na\\bigl(\\{i:g_i^x(k^f)&lt;q\\}\\bigr)&gt;\\frac34\n\\quad\\text{if }x\\text{ has a claim outside }I_+.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Every upward child keeps Min masses unchanged. Translate a fixed relevant Min witness \\(y\\) to that width-\\(q\\) box. All gaps at most \\(p\\) become Min claims, so those coordinates receive \\(-\\) and stay fixed. Every other coordinate has gap greater than \\(p\\) and moves upward by at most \\(p\\), leaving its new deviation strictly below the old peak. A gap-zero coordinate stays fixed, proving exact peak preservation.</p><p>At the start, \\(k^\\downarrow\\le c\\) and \\(y\\ge A\\), so \\(s_y\\le D/2\\). A Min claim at \\(i\\) gives\n\\[\nk_i^f\\le y_i+D/2\\le c_i+P,\n\\]\nso \\(i\\notin I_+\\).</p><p>Each \\(k_i\\) is nondecreasing during the upward pass while \\(s_y\\) is fixed. Hence\n\\[\ng_i^y(k^f)\\le g_i^y(k^\\downarrow).\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nThe set of Min gaps \\(&lt;q\\) can only grow. By Lemma 5.1 its \\(b\\)-mass was greater than \\(3/4\\) whenever \\(y\\) had a claim outside \\(I_-\\). Positivity of \\(b\\) proves the first final-pivot mass bound.</p><p>For Max, if \\(x\\) claims \\(j\\notin I_+\\), then\n\\[\nx_j\\ge A_j+D-P,\\qquad k_j^f\\le c_j+P,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\ns_x(k^f)\\ge D/2-2P&gt;D/2-M.\n\\]\nAn upper-capped coordinate has \\(k_i^f=c_i+M\\), and \\(x_i\\le A_i+D\\), giving deviation at most \\(D/2-M\\). Every maximizer is strictly below the upper cap.</p><p>If the \\(a\\)-mass of the gaps \\(&lt;q\\) were at most \\(3/4\\), the width-\\(q\\) translation would be a Max witness for the final child’s masses \\(\\frac43a\\). Its maximizers would receive \\(+\\) and force positive upward movement, contradicting the unchanged update.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.3, “Witness survival.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the nonbase input, witnesses, marks, provisional labels, and reweighted masses of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume all recursive children satisfy the simultaneous labelling guarantee.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every parent Max witness having a claim outside \\(I_+\\) remains a Max witness for \\((A,D,\\bar a,\\bar b)\\). Every parent Min witness having a claim outside \\(I_-\\) remains a Min witness for that input.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(r=D/2\\), the provisional width. Since\n\\[\nq=D/128&lt;r/32=D/64,\n\\]\nthe translated width-\\(r\\) witness claims every coordinate whose parent gap is \\(&lt;q\\).</p><p>For a Max witness \\(x\\) as stated, put\n\\[\nN_x=\\{i:g_i^x(k^f)&lt;q\\},\n\\qquad\nQ_+=\\{i:\\lambda_i^0=+\\}.\n\\]\nLemma 5.2 gives \\(a(N_x)&gt;3/4\\). Proposition 3 gives \\(N_x\\subseteq S_x\\), and every coordinate of \\(N_x\\) is a claim of the width-\\(r\\) translation, which qualifies under unchanged masses \\(a\\). Correctness of the provisional child yields \\(N_x\\subseteq Q_+\\). Consequently,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bar a(S_x)\n&amp;=\\frac85a(S_x)-\\frac45a(S_x\\cap Q_+)\\\\\n&amp;&lt;\\frac85\\cdot1-\\frac45\\cdot\\frac34\n=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe continuation uses the same box and operator, so the subsolution inequalities are unchanged.</p><p>For Min, put\n\\[\nN_y=\\{i:g_i^y(k^f)&lt;q\\},\n\\qquad\nQ_-=\\{i:\\lambda_i^0=-\\}.\n\\]\nThe same arguments give\n\\[\nN_y\\subseteq S_y\\cap Q_-,\n\\qquad b(N_y)&gt;3/4.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\bar b(S_y)\n=\n\\frac85b(S_y)-\\frac45b(S_y\\cap Q_-)\n&lt;\n\\frac85-\\frac45\\cdot\\frac34\n=1.\n\\]\nThe supersolution inequalities remain unchanged, so \\(y\\) survives as well.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemma 6.1 and Sections 6.1–6.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a game of complete explicit binary input length \\(L\\), with \\(n\\ge1\\) vertices, \\(m=|E|\\), and transformed weights\n  \\[\n  \\widehat w(e)=(n+1)w(e)+1,\\qquad\n  W=\\max_e|\\widehat w(e)|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  D_0=\\min\\{2^d:2^d\\ge64(n+1)(W+1),\\ d\\ge0\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Run the procedure of Proposition 4 on\n  \\[\n  A=0,\\qquad D=D_0,\\qquad a_i=b_i=1/n.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use\n  \\[\n  \\rho=32/25,\\qquad\n  B(\\mu)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  0,&amp;\\mu&gt;1,\\\\\n  1+\\lfloor\\log_\\rho(1/\\mu)\\rfloor,&amp;0&lt;\\mu\\le1.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  d_0=\\log_2D_0,\\qquad\n  B_0=B(1/n^2),\\qquad\n  H=d_0+B_0,\\qquad\n  s=2048n+3.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The complete \\(\\mathrm{Label}\\) computation, including preprocessing, exact arithmetic, recursion, and output of the \\(n\\) labels, has a uniform deterministic implementation using\n\\[\n2^{O((\\log_2(L+2))^2)}\n\\]\nbit operations.</p>", "proof": "<p>The explicit encoding bounds \\(n\\) and \\(m\\) polynomially in \\(L+1\\), and every original weight has \\(O(L+1)\\) bits. The perturbation and definition of \\(D_0\\) imply\n\\[\n\\log_2(W+1)=O(L+\\log(n+1)),\n\\qquad\nd_0=O(L+\\log(n+1)).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\rho&gt;1\\) is fixed,\n\\[\nB_0=1+\\lfloor\\log_\\rho(n^2)\\rfloor\n=O(1+\\log(n+1)).\n\\]\nThus \\(H,s\\) are polynomial in \\(L+1\\), and \\(B_0=O(\\log(L+2))\\). Lemma 6.1 gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_2(\\text{number of calls})\n&amp;\\le\n\\log_2(H+1)+\\log_2(B_0+1)\n+B_0\\log_2(1+Hs)\\\\\n&amp;=O((\\log_2(L+2))^2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis includes \\(n=1\\), where \\(B_0=1\\).</p><p>By the nested-box property of Proposition 4, every endpoint and pivot lies in \\([0,D_0]^V\\). Each coordinate and width uses \\(O(d_0+1)\\) bits. In a basic iteration, an intermediate edge expression is a coordinate plus a transformed weight. It may be negative, but its signed binary length is\n\\[\nO(d_0+\\log(W+1)+1),\n\\]\npolynomial in \\(L+1\\).</p><p>Represent a mass by a positive numerator and denominator, without reducing fractions. Starting from \\(1/n\\), each mass is multiplied along a path only by members of\n\\[\n\\{1,4/3,4/5,8/5\\}.\n\\]\nOnly budget-decreasing edges change masses, and there are at most \\(B_0\\) such edges on a path. Every numerator and denominator therefore has\n\\[\nO(B_0+\\log(n+1))\n\\]\nbits. Children receive their own input-mass copies; completing a sibling does not change the parent’s masses. Testing \\(a_i b_i&gt;1\\) uses integer cross-multiplication, and all other mass operations use polynomial-length integers.</p><p>The proof quantities \\(B,\\mu\\), peaks, gaps, supports, and witness vectors need not be computed or stored. The algorithm evaluates \\(F\\) only inside \\(\\mathrm{Basic}\\), using explicit max/min scans of the edge list.</p><p>Each call has polynomial nonrecursive work. In the width base case,\n\\[\nD&lt;4096,\n\\]\nand Proposition 1 bounds the number of operator evaluations, including the final stability test, by\n\\[\n1+nD\\le1+4095n.\n\\]\nEach evaluation scans the \\(m\\) edges, adds transformed weights, takes extrema, and clips at \\(n\\) endpoints. The midpoint test is implemented as\n\\[\n2z_i&gt;2A_i+D,\n\\]\nalso covering \\(D=1\\).</p><p>At a nonbase call, the pivot constants are obtained by exact division by powers of two. Each pass has \\(O(n+1)\\) iterations apart from child work, and each update is a scan of \\(n\\) coordinates. Preparing child inputs, recording marks, reweighting, and applying overrides all use polynomially many operations on polynomial-length integers. Schoolbook arithmetic suffices.</p><p>A depth-first implementation runs children sequentially, keeping one polynomial-size record for each active call. There are at most \\(H+1\\) active records. Stack management is included in the same polynomial local bound. Computing transformed weights, finding \\(D_0\\), creating masses \\(1/n\\), reading the input, and producing labels take polynomial time.</p><p>Multiplying the polynomial per-call cost by the call count gives\n\\[\n2^{C(\\log_2(L+2))^2}\n\\]\nbit operations for an absolute constant \\(C\\). All constants and arithmetic and control operations are fixed, giving a uniform deterministic bit algorithm.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1, “Number of calls.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Inherit the main computation and \\(H,B_0,s\\) of Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The total number of calls, including the initial call, is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell=0}^{H}\n\\sum_{j=0}^{\\min(\\ell,B_0)}\n\\binom{\\ell}{j}s^j\n\\le\n(H+1)(B_0+1)(1+Hs)^{B_0}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 4, every call-tree path has at most \\(H\\) edges. At most \\(B_0\\) edges decrease \\(B\\), even if some decrease it by more than one.</p><p>At each node, the provisional child is the only child preserving \\(B\\). The two passes provide at most \\(2(1024n+1)\\) budget-decreasing children, and the continuation provides one, for a total at most \\(s\\).</p><p>Consider a node at depth \\(\\ell\\) whose path uses \\(j\\) budget-decreasing edges. Their positions can be chosen in \\(\\binom{\\ell}{j}\\) ways. Order each node’s budget-decreasing children by execution order. At each chosen position there are at most \\(s\\) choices of ordinal; at every other position there is at most one choice. This specifies at most \\(\\binom{\\ell}{j}s^j\\) nodes. Sum over all permitted \\(\\ell,j\\).</p><p>Each summand is at most\n\\[\n(Hs)^j\\le(1+Hs)^{B_0},\n\\]\nand there are at most \\((H+1)(B_0+1)\\) summands.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "the “Positional values” and “Threshold queries and exact reconstruction” parts of the proof of Corollary 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G=(V,E)\\) be a finite total game with \\(n\\ge1\\) vertices, signed integer weights \\(w\\), arbitrary history-dependent strategies, and payoff \\(\\operatorname{MP}_w\\) defined by the lower limiting average.</p><p class=\"li\">Self-loops and explicitly labelled parallel edges are allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  W_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\max_e|w(e)|,\n  \\qquad\n  K=1+\\left\\lceil\n  \\log_2\\bigl(2(W_{\\mathrm{in}}+1)n^2\\bigr)\n  \\right\\rceil.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A restricted game \\(H\\) is obtained by removing outgoing choices while retaining at least one outgoing edge at every vertex.</p><p class=\"li\">For integer \\(p\\) and positive integer \\(q\\), a threshold query for \\(H\\) means obtaining the complete zero-threshold winning set after replacing its weights by\n  \\[\n  w_{p/q}(e)=qw(e)-p.\n  \\]\n  In the algorithmic assertion below, assume these queries are answered correctly.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist globally optimal positional strategies for both players, simultaneously from every vertex and against arbitrary history-dependent opponents. Every value of \\(G\\), and every value in any restricted game, is a reduced rational \\(a/b\\) satisfying\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n,\\qquad\n|a|\\le bW_{\\mathrm{in}},\n\\qquad\n-W_{\\mathrm{in}}\\le a/b\\le W_{\\mathrm{in}}.\n\\]</p><p>A procedure \\(\\mathrm{Values}(H)\\) computes the entire exact reduced-rational value vector of any restricted game using at most \\(nK\\) threshold queries. The original \\(n,W_{\\mathrm{in}},K\\) may be used for every such game. The queried games have binary encoding length polynomial in the original input length, and the work outside the queries is polynomial.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.1 supplies uniform positional attainment and the rational bounds.</p><p>For every infinite play,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{MP}_{w_{p/q}}(\\pi)\n=\nq\\operatorname{MP}_w(\\pi)-p.\n\\]\nPositional attainment makes the winning set of the transformed game exactly\n\\[\n\\{v:\\operatorname{val}_w(v)\\ge p/q\\}.\n\\]</p><p>For each vertex, start with the closed interval\n\\[\n[-W_{\\mathrm{in}},W_{\\mathrm{in}}]\n\\]\nand make \\(K\\) bisections. Query the midpoint. If the vertex belongs to the returned winning set, move the lower endpoint to the midpoint; otherwise move the upper endpoint. The final interval contains the value and has width\n\\[\n2W_{\\mathrm{in}}2^{-K}&lt;1/n^2.\n\\]\nDistinct reduced rationals with positive denominators at most \\(n\\) differ by at least \\(1/n^2\\).</p><p>For a final interval \\([\\ell,h]\\), test, for every \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), the integer\n\\[\na=\\lceil b\\ell\\rceil\n\\]\nfor \\(a/b\\le h\\), reduce retained fractions, and remove repetitions. Since \\(b(h-\\ell)&lt;1\\), every possible numerator is tested. The value bounds guarantee exactly one resulting rational.</p><p>This uses at most \\(nK\\) threshold queries. Every midpoint has denominator dividing \\(2^K\\) and numerator of\n\\[\nO(\\log(W_{\\mathrm{in}}+1)+K)\n\\]\nbits. The transformed edge weights have the same order of bit length. If the original input length is \\(L\\), then\n\\[\nK=O(L+\\log(n+1)),\n\\]\nand each queried game has encoding length \\(O(L+mK)\\), polynomial in \\(L+1\\). Reconstruction, rational comparison, and copying restricted edge lists have polynomial bit cost.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "“Positional values” in the proof of Corollary 7.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Inherit the finite total game, \\(n\\), and \\(W_{\\mathrm{in}}\\) of Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both players have one positional strategy attaining their values from every vertex simultaneously, against arbitrary history-dependent opponents. Every value has a reduced representation \\(a/b\\) with\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n,\\qquad |a|\\le bW_{\\mathrm{in}}.\n\\]\nThe same statements hold after restricting choices while retaining a total game.</p>", "proof": "<p>The uniform form of Ehrenfeucht–Mycielski positional determinacy [13], stated explicitly in [7, Theorem 1 and the following paragraph], gives the assertion for the edge-relation convention without parallel entries.</p><p>For the present convention, replace every explicit edge \\(e:u\\to v\\) by\n\\[\nu\\to x_e\\to v\n\\]\nthrough a fresh vertex with one outgoing edge, assigning weights \\(2w(e)\\) and \\(0\\). Retain ownership at original vertices; the owner of \\(x_e\\) is immaterial. The expanded graph has neither parallel edges nor loops. Expanded histories retain the identity of each original edge. Their averages at even prefixes equal the original averages, while the odd-prefix discrepancy tends to zero. Payoffs and uniform positional guarantees therefore transfer back, including loops and parallel entries.</p><p>Fix both uniform optimal positional strategies. From any start, the resulting play eventually repeats a simple directed cycle of at most \\(n\\) edges in the original graph, and its mean is the value there. This gives the stated numerator and denominator bounds and the interval \\([-W_{\\mathrm{in}},W_{\\mathrm{in}}]\\). Restricting choices leaves the same bounds valid as long as every vertex retains an outgoing edge.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "“Separate strategy self-reductions” and “Call and bit bounds” in the proof of Corollary 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite total signed-integer-weight game \\(G\\) with \\(n\\) vertices, \\(m\\) explicitly listed edges, and complete binary input length \\(L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathbf v=(\\operatorname{val}_w(v))_{v\\in V}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume correct access to the threshold-query interface of Proposition 7, and use its procedure \\(\\mathrm{Values}(H)\\) on restricted games.</p><p class=\"li\">Use\n  \\[\n  W_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\max_e|w(e)|,\\qquad\n  K=1+\\left\\lceil\\log_2(2(W_{\\mathrm{in}}+1)n^2)\\right\\rceil.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can recover globally optimal positional strategies for Max and Min by two separate sequences of value-preserving restrictions, each starting from the original game.</p><p>Including the initial value-vector computation, the full reduction uses at most\n\\[\nQ=nK(1+m)\n\\]\nthreshold calls. Every queried game has polynomial encoding length in \\(L+1\\), and all work outside the threshold calls, including output, has polynomial bit cost.</p>", "proof": "<p>An edge joining equal-valued vertices need not be optimal. For example, a Max vertex \\(u\\) with a zero-weight self-loop and a zero-weight edge to a vertex \\(v\\) whose only edge is a self-loop of weight \\(1\\) has value \\(1\\), as does \\(v\\), but staying at \\(u\\) yields payoff \\(0\\). Thus restrictions must preserve the entire value vector.</p><p>First compute \\(\\mathbf v=\\mathrm{Values}(G)\\). For Max, start with a fresh copy \\(H=G\\) and process Max vertices in a fixed order. At a vertex \\(u\\), for each outgoing edge \\(e\\), form the trial \\(H_e\\) retaining only \\(e\\) at \\(u\\). Keep a trial with\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Values}(H_e)=\\mathbf v,\n\\]\nand continue from it.</p><p>Such an edge exists by the uniform positional attainment in Proposition 7. Take the edge chosen at \\(u\\) by a globally optimal positional Max strategy of the current \\(H\\). Restricting Max’s choices cannot increase any value, while that strategy remains available and secures the entire current value vector, so no value decreases. This preserves the invariant \\(\\mathrm{Values}(H)=\\mathbf v\\).</p><p>At the end, Max has one allowed edge at each owned vertex and Min retains all original choices. The resulting single Max strategy secures \\(\\mathbf v\\) from every start against every Min strategy.</p><p>For Min, start again from a fresh copy of the original game, not the Max-restricted game. At each Min vertex retain an edge whose trial preserves \\(\\mathbf v\\). A globally optimal positional Min strategy of the current game supplies such an edge: restricting Min cannot decrease values, while that strategy prevents increases. The final Min strategy is optimal against every original Max strategy.</p><p>At a vertex, at most its original outdegree many trials are tested. The two self-reductions together therefore use at most \\(m\\) trial value-vector computations. Including the initial computation and using Proposition 7 gives at most\n\\[\nnK(1+m)=Q\n\\]\nthreshold calls. Proposition 7 gives polynomial query lengths and polynomial reconstruction and comparison costs. The explicit encoding bounds \\(n,m\\), and hence \\(Q\\), polynomially in \\(L+1\\). Value and strategy outputs also have polynomial length.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "“Fixed credit by resetting” in the proof of Corollary 8.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite total game \\(G=(V,E)\\), with ownership partition, signed integer weights \\(w\\), and arbitrary history-dependent opposition.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(v\\in V\\) and \\(c\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U(H)\\) denote the zero-threshold winning set of a mean-payoff game \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Construct \\(R_{v,c}\\) by replacing every \\(u\\in V\\) with:</p><p class=\"li l2\">an entry \\(u^{\\mathrm{in}}\\), owned by Min;</p><p class=\"li l2\">an exit \\(u^{\\mathrm{out}}\\), owned by the original owner of \\(u\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Add edges\n  \\[\n  u^{\\mathrm{in}}\\xrightarrow{\\,c\\,}v^{\\mathrm{in}},\n  \\qquad\n  u^{\\mathrm{in}}\\xrightarrow{\\,0\\,}u^{\\mathrm{out}},\n  \\]\n  and replace each labelled original edge \\(e:u\\to z\\) by its own edge\n  \\[\n  u^{\\mathrm{out}}\\xrightarrow{\\,w(e)\\,}z^{\\mathrm{in}}.\n  \\]\n  Start at \\(v^{\\mathrm{in}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Energy-winning means maintaining\n  \\[\n  c+\\sum_{t=0}^{j-1}w(e_t)\\ge0\n  \\quad\\text{for all }j\\ge0.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The reset game is total, with \\(2n\\) vertices and \\(m+2n\\) edges, and\n\\[\n(v,c)\\text{ is energy-winning in }G\n\\iff\nv^{\\mathrm{in}}\\in U(R_{v,c}).\n\\]</p><p>Whenever a positional Max strategy in \\(R_{v,c}\\) guarantees nonnegative mean payoff from \\(v^{\\mathrm{in}}\\), its choices at original Max exits define a positional energy-winning policy in \\(G\\) from \\((v,c)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The reset at \\(u=v\\) is a nonnegative self-loop, including when \\(c=0\\). Original parallel edges remain distinct. The construction is total and has the stated size.</p><p>For the forward implication, use an energy-winning strategy from \\((v,c)\\), restarting its history after every reset. In a transformed prefix, each completed segment has original-edge sum at least \\(-c\\), followed by reset weight \\(+c\\), so has nonnegative total. The unfinished segment has sum at least \\(-c\\). Intervening zero edges do not change the bound. Every transformed prefix sum is therefore at least \\(-c\\). Dividing by its length gives nonnegative lower limiting average. This includes no resets and infinitely many resets, and does not assume the original energy strategy is positional.</p><p>Conversely, suppose \\(v^{\\mathrm{in}}\\in U(R_{v,c})\\). Uniform positional attainment, as recorded in Proposition 7, permits a positional Max strategy guaranteeing nonnegative mean payoff from that start. Restrict its choices at Max exits to a positional policy in \\(G\\).</p><p>If a consistent original prefix of length \\(j\\) from \\(v\\) had weight \\(q&lt;-c\\), Min could choose continuation at the entries, follow that prefix at its owned exits, and then reset. This forms a closed walk from \\(v^{\\mathrm{in}}\\) of weight \\(q+c&lt;0\\) and length \\(2j+1\\). Positionality of Max makes the walk consistent on every repetition. Min could repeat it forever, giving mean payoff\n\\[\n\\frac{q+c}{2j+1}&lt;0,\n\\]\na contradiction. Every original prefix thus has weight at least \\(-c\\). This proves the equivalence and policy assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "the finite-credit bound, equations (8.3)–(8.4), in the proof of Corollary 8.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite total game \\(G\\) with \\(n\\ge1\\), signed integer weights \\(w\\), and zero-threshold winning set \\(U(G)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  W_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\max_e|w(e)|,\n  \\qquad\n  H=(n-1)W_{\\mathrm{in}}.\n  \\]\n  Both quantities may be zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c_{\\min}(v)\\) be the least nonnegative integer energy-winning credit, or \\(\\infty\\) if none exists.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every vertex \\(v\\),\n\\[\nc_{\\min}(v)&lt;\\infty\n\\iff\nv\\in U(G),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nv\\in U(G)\\Longrightarrow 0\\le c_{\\min}(v)\\le H.\n\\]</p><p>There exists a positional Max policy on the original winning region under which every consistent prefix from a winning start has weight at least \\(-H\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>An energy winner with finite credit is a zero-threshold mean-payoff winner because its prefix sums are bounded below.</p><p>Conversely, Proposition 2 identifies \\(U(G)\\) with its high band and supplies a positional Max policy whose permitted simple cycles all have nonnegative original sum. Delete simple cycles from any consistent prefix. The remaining walk has at most \\(n-1\\) edges, each of weight at least \\(-W_{\\mathrm{in}}\\), so the original prefix has weight at least\n\\[\n-(n-1)W_{\\mathrm{in}}=-H.\n\\]\nCredit \\(H\\) therefore suffices at every winning start.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "“Integer data and homogeneous supports” in the proof of Corollary 9.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C,D\\) be explicitly listed matrices over \\(\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{-\\infty\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider the homogeneous system\n  \\[\n  \\max_j(C_{ij}+z_j)\n  \\le\n  \\max_j(D_{ij}+z_j)\n  \\qquad\\text{for every row }i,\n  \\]\n  with \\(z_j\\in\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(a+(-\\infty)=-\\infty\\), \\(\\max\\varnothing=-\\infty\\), and support \\(\\{j:z_j\\ne-\\infty\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L\\) be the full explicit binary input length.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Polynomial-bit preprocessing reduces the computation of the maximal support to the zero-threshold winning column vertices of a total, signed-integer-weight mean-payoff game of polynomial explicit encoding length, with any forced-\\(-\\infty\\) coordinates excluded.</p><p>The maximal support is attained by a solution; the identically \\(-\\infty\\) solution realizes the empty case.</p><p>For rational input data, clearing denominators by multiplying every finite coefficient and variable by the product of positive input denominators preserves inequalities and finite-coordinate supports. The same scaling preserves comparisons of permutation sums defining tropical rank and yields integer data of polynomial binary length.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(Q\\) be the product of the positive denominators of the finite input coefficients, with \\(Q=1\\) if there are none. Multiplying every finite coefficient and variable by \\(Q\\) preserves inequalities and finite-coordinate supports. It also preserves comparisons of permutation sums. The bit length of \\(Q\\) is at most one plus the sum of denominator bit lengths, so the integer data have polynomial binary length.</p><p>For the integer homogeneous system, apply the preprocessing of Akian, Gaubert, and Guterman [1, Section 2.1]. Discard a row whose left side is identically \\(-\\infty\\). If a right side is identically \\(-\\infty\\), every variable with a finite coefficient on that row’s left side must equal \\(-\\infty\\). Record those coordinates, substitute \\(-\\infty\\) throughout, and repeat. Every step removes a row or a variable, so the work is polynomial.</p><p>If no variables remain, the maximal support is empty. Otherwise, add tautologies \\(z_j\\le z_j\\) for remaining variables.</p><p>The resulting bipartite game has a Min vertex for each remaining column and a Max vertex for each row. A finite \\(C_{ij}\\) gives an edge from column \\(j\\) to row \\(i\\) of weight \\(-C_{ij}\\); a finite \\(D_{ij}\\) gives an edge from row \\(i\\) to column \\(j\\) of weight \\(D_{ij}\\). Every vertex has an outgoing edge.</p><p>The external support characterization [1, Theorem 3.2] identifies winning column vertices with the maximal support of a solution. Deleted coordinates belong to no support. The union of all supports is attained because coordinatewise maxima of finitely many solutions are again solutions. The identically \\(-\\infty\\) vector is a solution and realizes the empty case.</p><p>The cited game counts a column–row–column pair as one turn. Counting its edges separately halves the mean payoff, while an unmatched edge contributes a discrepancy tending to zero. The source uses upper limiting averages; positional optimality makes its nonnegative winning set coincide with that for lower limiting averages.</p><p>The game has polynomially many vertices and edges, and its weights are copied or negated from scaled coefficients or equal to zero. Its full encoding length is polynomial in \\(L+1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "“Affine and finite feasibility” in the proof of Corollary 9.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A,B,c,d\\) be rational or \\(-\\infty\\) data for\n  \\[\n  \\max\\{c_i,\\max_j(A_{ij}+x_j)\\}\n  \\le\n  \\max\\{d_i,\\max_j(B_{ij}+x_j)\\}\n  \\quad\\text{for every }i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Variables lie in \\(\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Clear denominators as in Proposition 11.</p><p class=\"li\">Append \\(c\\) to \\(A\\) and \\(d\\) to \\(B\\) as a new column, introducing a coordinate \\(t\\). The resulting homogeneous system is\n  \\[\n  \\max\\{c_i+t,\\max_j(A_{ij}+z_j)\\}\n  \\le\n  \\max\\{d_i+t,\\max_j(B_{ij}+z_j)\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\) be its maximal finite-coordinate support.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The affine system is feasible exactly when \\(t\\in S\\). It has a solution with all original coordinates finite exactly when \\(S\\) contains all \\(n+1\\) coordinates.</p><p>Equalities are handled by imposing both inequalities. A max-atom \\(z\\le\\max(x,y)+a\\) is a homogeneous row with one left term and two right terms, so finite-variable feasibility reduces to the all-finite support test.</p><p>For rational max-atom data, a finite real solution exists exactly when a finite rational solution exists.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every affine solution \\(x\\) gives a homogeneous solution \\((x,0)\\). Conversely, a homogeneous solution with finite \\(t\\) gives the affine solution\n\\[\nx_j=z_j-t.\n\\]\nBy the attained maximal-support property in Proposition 11, the stated support tests are equivalent to the required existence assertions.</p><p>A coordinate eliminated during preprocessing is absent from the support, so any test requiring it fails; this includes \\(t\\) if forced to \\(-\\infty\\). This is the homogenization underlying [1, Theorem 3.5 and Corollaries 3.4 and 3.7].</p><p>Equalities are pairs of inequalities. A max-atom has the stated homogeneous form, to which the all-finite test applies.</p><p>For integer coefficients, flooring every finite coordinate preserves each inequality: flooring commutes with integer translation and with finite maxima. Scaling back gives a rational solution whenever a real one exists, as in [1, Proposition 3.9, Corollary 3.10, and Remark 3.15].</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "“Fixed corank” in the proof of Corollary 9.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M^{\\mathrm{all}}\\) be an explicitly listed \\(m\\times n\\) matrix over \\(\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{-\\infty\\}\\), with \\(m\\ge n\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\ge0\\) be fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Tropical rank is the largest order of a square submatrix with a finite, uniquely maximizing permutation sum, or zero if none exists.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(n&gt;k\\), put \\(r=n-k\\). For each set of \\(r\\) columns, let \\(M\\) be the selected \\(m\\times r\\) matrix.</p><p class=\"li\">For this selected matrix, consider\n  \\[\n  M_{ij}+z_j\n  \\le\n  \\max_{\\ell\\ne j}(M_{i\\ell}+z_\\ell)\n  \\qquad\n  (1\\le i\\le m,\\ 1\\le j\\le r).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(n\\le k\\), the rank threshold \\(n-k\\) is nonpositive and the answer is yes.</p><p>If \\(n&gt;k\\), the rank is at least \\(r\\) exactly when at least one selected set of \\(r\\) columns has empty maximal support for the displayed homogeneous system. Thus the rank test reduces to at most\n\\[\n\\binom nr=\\binom nk\\le n^k\n\\]\nhomogeneous-support computations, each on an instance of polynomial encoding length.</p>", "proof": "<p>The nonpositive-threshold case is immediate.</p><p>For \\(r=n-k&gt;0\\), the rank criterion [1, Corollaries 4.16–4.17] says that rank is at least \\(r\\) exactly when some \\(r\\) columns are tropically independent.</p><p>The selected columns are dependent precisely when there is a vector \\(z\\not\\equiv-\\infty\\) such that every row maximum is attained at least twice or equals \\(-\\infty\\). Equivalently, \\(z\\) satisfies\n\\[\nM_{ij}+z_j\n\\le\n\\max_{\\ell\\ne j}(M_{i\\ell}+z_\\ell)\n\\]\nfor every \\(i,j\\). Hence the maximal support is empty exactly when the selected columns are independent. This is the inequality form of the game reduction in [1, Theorem 4.9].</p><p>The row index \\((i,j)\\) retains the column excluded from Max’s next choice. Empty right sides and all-\\(-\\infty\\) columns are covered by Proposition 11, including \\(r=1\\).</p><p>There are at most \\(mr\\) inequalities, with \\(r\\) coefficients on each side, so each test has polynomial encoding length. There are \\(\\binom nr=\\binom nk\\le n^k\\) selections. Rational normalization preserves the rank comparisons by Proposition 11.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [0, 3, "cite"], [0, 4, "cite"], [1, 2, "cite"], [1, 3, "cite"], [5, 0, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [5, 10, "cite"], [5, 11, "cite"], [5, 15, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 21, "cite"], [7, 12, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [8, 15, "cite"], [8, 16, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [15, 0, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 1, "cite"], [17, 2, "cite"], [17, 19, "cite"], [17, 20, "cite"], [18, 17, "cite"], [19, 1, "cite"], [19, 2, "cite"], [20, 3, "cite"], [21, 3, "cite"], [22, 4, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [22, 24, "cite"], [23, 4, "cite"], [24, 4, "cite"]]});
